{"role":"assistant","content":"Задача понятна: часы показывают свои зональные границы вместо запланированных коридоров пульса. Прежде чем править генератор, проверю в документации Intervals.icu, какой формат `workout_doc` реально приводит к пользовательским диапазонам bpm на часах, а не к зонам.","tool_calls":[{"type":"function","id":"tool_Pv5nd61MNMQjsSpBIa7Gf2VQ","function":{"name":"WebSearch","arguments":"{\"query\":\"intervals.icu API workout_doc steps target heart rate format units bpm\"}"}},{"type":"function","id":"tool_ONspxM1HUVqB5vB0ROfdrB4F","function":{"name":"WebSearch","arguments":"{\"query\":\"intervals.icu workout JSON steps \\\"target\\\" \\\"%lthr\\\" custom range Garmin export zones\"}"}}]}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_ONspxM1HUVqB5vB0ROfdrB4F","content":"Title: platypus45/domestique: Adaptive cycling training planner\nSite: Github\nDate: 2026-10-23\nURL: https://github.com/platypus45/domestique\nSnippet: Switch the target mode in Settings (Power ↔ Heart rate). The heart-rate option unlocks once your LTHR (lactate-threshold heart rate) is known — Domestique ...\n\n---\n\nTitle: Run Activity Heart Rate Alerts Do Not Match Configured LTHR Zones (v23.18)\nSite: Garmin Forums\nDate: 2025-08-18\nURL: https://forums.garmin.com/outdoor-recreation/outdoor-recreation/f/fenix-7-series/419555/run-activity-heart-rate-alerts-do-not-match-configured-lthr-zones-v23-18\nSnippet: In its current state, this bug renders the heart-rate-based Daily Suggestions feature not just useless, but actively harmful to a user's training goals. Garmin - LF Hello JordiGT I am sorry to hear you are having trouble with the Daily Suggested workout. You mentioned the HR zones are based around you %LTHR. So you have auto detect enabled for this? When using the Auto Detection feature on your Garmin device, Daily Suggested Workouts will use your lactate threshold and functional threshold power\n\n---\n\nTitle: intervals-mcp by ale7canna\nSite: glama.ai\nDate: 2026-08-20\nURL: https://glama.ai/mcp/servers/ale7canna/intervals-mcp\nSnippet: Come arrivano gli allenamenti sul Garmin 1. `create_workout` scrive un evento `category: \"WORKOUT\"` sul calendario; il campo `description` contiene l'allenamento nella sintassi testuale di intervals.icu. 2. intervals.icu lo interpreta e ne costruisce la struttura ( `workout_doc`). 3. Se su intervals.icu → Settings, nel box Garmin, è attivo Upload planned workouts (con Garmin Connect autorizzato), i prossimi giorni di calendario vengono caricati su Garmin Connect e da lì arrivano all'orologio. 4.\n\n---\n\nTitle: garmin-mcp-bridge | MCP Servers\nSite: lobehub.com\nDate: 2026-07-19\nURL: https://lobehub.com/it/mcp/jonas-theobald-garmin-mcp-bridge\nSnippet: Writing workouts Permalink for this section The workout_doc trap Permalink for this section Never include a `workout_doc` field in the payload. A planned workout only syncs to your watch if Intervals.icu parsed the description into structured steps. What triggers that parsing is undocumented, and the intuitive guess is wrong: sending `workout_doc: {}` to _request_ parsing actually suppresses it. The server reads the presence of the field as \"steps already supplied\" and skips the parser. The work\n\n---\n\nTitle: Intervals.icu MCP Server by oscarantxo-web\nSite: glama.ai\nDate: 2026-09-20\nURL: https://glama.ai/mcp/servers/oscarantxo-web/intervals-mcp-server\nSnippet: The first sentence names the action ('Post event'), the target resource (Intervals.icu), and the audience ('for an athlete'), then clarifies the create-vs ...\n\n---\n\nTitle: Exact HR corridors on Garmin from Intervals.icu API workouts — start/end, not value/end\nSite: Worklore\nDate: 2026-05-12\nURL: https://worklore.dev/s/2026-05-12-exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts\nSnippet: Reproduce this Prerequisites: an Intervals.icu account with API access (OAuth token or API key), athlete thresholds configured in Intervals (LTHR at minimum), and a Garmin watch that syncs workouts from Intervals via Garmin Connect. Your agent will need from you: your Intervals athlete id and API credential; your LTHR as stored in Intervals; permission to create (and later delete) a test calendar event. Steps: 1. Create an event via `POST /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events` with `workout_doc.steps`, wh\n\n---\n\nTitle: Intervals Workout · GarminFans\nSite: GarminFans\nDate: 2026-11-07\nURL: https://www.garminfans.com/fi/apps/8f9714b5-9367-4424-b086-30711f0d9632/\nSnippet: Kuvaus Intervals Workout is a Data Field for Edge 540 and 1050 that reads your Intervals.icu structured workout and guides you in real time, step by step. At a glance: • Current target watts (blue) + 3s actual power (green/red at ±15 W) • Heart rate color-coded by zone (gray, blue, green, orange, red by % of threshold) • Cadence with target (e.g. \"target 90 rpm\") — green ±5, orange ±10, red beyond • Step name + position (e.g. \"Ramp 50 to 75 %FTP 1/10\") • Step time remaining + total time • Per-st\n\n---\n\nTitle: Coach help — PaceTrack\nSite: PaceTrack\nDate: 2026-06-15\nURL: https://pacetrack.cc/help.php\nSnippet: 9. Athlete Profile 9.12. Athlete Training Zones The \"Training zones\" link in a registered athlete's profile opens `/athlete/training-zones.php` on their behalf — here you can view and edit their running HR and pace zones. - Z1–Z5 HR zones: min/max heart rate for each zone (Recovery → Maximum). - Z1–Z5 pace zones: \"slower\" and \"faster\" (mm:ss/km) for each zone. - \"Calculate & import\" panel: enter anchor values (max HR, LTHR, threshold pace) and auto-calculate zone boundaries from presets; the \"Lo\n\n---\n\nTitle: Athlete help — PaceTrack\nSite: PaceTrack\nDate: 2026-11-01\nURL: https://pacetrack.cc/en/help-athlete.php\nSnippet: 8. Profile Copy link to this section “Profile” Tab - Training Zones — a link to `/athlete/training-zones.php` where you set running HR zones (Z1–Z5, Recovery → Maximum) and pace zones (Z1–Z5, mm:ss/km). Used by your coach when building structured workouts with zone-based steps. You can set values manually, calculate them from anchor values (max HR, LTHR, threshold pace), or import directly from Garmin Connect (only when it is connected the previous way, see 7.1) or Intervals.icu (if connected, s\n\n---\n\nTitle: Garmin preset for Heart Rate Zones (%LTHR based) - Feature Requests - Intervals.icu Forum\nSite: Intervals.icu Forum\nDate: 2022-11-21\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/garmin-preset-for-heart-rate-zones-lthr-based/24197\nSnippet: post by ggarcea on Nov 21, 2022 Hello! I’m matching the heart rate and power zones to my Garmin 955 and I can’t seem to find a preset that matches the Garmin default zones based on %LTHR. For power, Garmin seems to match perfectly the Friel preset for cycling and the MyProCoach Run for running. Would it be possible to add it (it seems to be the same for cycling, running and generic sport zones)? Or am I missing a preset that matches? These are the default Garmin Zones: Garmin LTHR Zones Thanks! \n\n---\n\nTitle: Z2 high limit / 85/89% LTHR - Training - Intervals.icu Forum\nSite: Intervals.icu Forum\nDate: 2021-04-15\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/z2-high-limit-85-89-lthr/3670\nSnippet: post by TheTrVeHerve on Apr 15, 2021 TheTrVeHerve's profile Hello ! I like Intervals.icu more than Strava for workout analysis, the datas are clear, it’s easy to setup and very understandable. I find the fitness curve much more real than with other models and the TFP model does not increase my real FTP like Garmin ! Regarding clycling, power model is perfect. For running, which is not in watts but in %LTHR, I’m curious about zone 2 : it’s 85/89% LTHR… I find it high. In my case it’s 135/143, but\n\n---\n\nTitle: Anaerobic Threshold Calculator – LTHR & Training Zones\nSite: CalculatorMom\nDate: 2026-02-09\nURL: https://calculatormom.com/anaerobic-threshold-calculator/\nSnippet: Ventilatory Threshold ≈ 168 bpm Breathing Break. Talk TestFail MarkerVT2 Point where breathing becomes labored and rhythmic speech is impossible. Closely tracks AT. Glycogen Demand High Fuel Source. Carbs~90% Fats~10% At threshold, your body shifts to burning almost exclusively stored glycogen (carbs). Cardiac Drift Risk Environment Dependent HR Decoupling. EffectRise CauseHeat/Fatigue Likelihood of heart rate rising over time even if pace/effort remains constant. RPE Score 7-8/10 Perceived Exer\n\nWhen you rely on a result in your answer, cite it inline as a markdown link, e.g. [title](url)."}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_Pv5nd61MNMQjsSpBIa7Gf2VQ","content":"Title: domestique/README.md at clean-main · platypus45/domestique\nSite: Github\nDate: 2026-07-26\nURL: https://github.com/platypus45/domestique/blob/clean-main/README.md\nSnippet: Train by heart rate (no power meter) Permalink: Train by heart rate (no power meter) No power meter? Domestique can prescribe every workout in beats per minute instead of watts. - Switch the target mode in Settings (Power ↔ Heart rate). The heart-rate option unlocks once your LTHR (lactate-threshold heart rate) is known — Domestique keeps it synced automatically from your intervals.icu ride data, unless you've set it manually, in which case your number stays put. - Steady aerobic work gets real \n\n---\n\nTitle: GitHub - platypus45/domestique: Adaptive cycling training planner: 7 science-grounded injury-prevention guardrails close the loop between planned and actual training. 4,232 ZWO workouts, 622 routes, h\nSite: Github\nDate: 2026-10-23\nURL: https://github.com/platypus45/domestique\nSnippet: Train by heart rate (no power meter) - Switch the target mode in Settings (Power ↔ Heart rate). The heart-rate option unlocks once your LTHR (lactate-threshold heart rate) is known — Domestique keeps it synced automatically from your intervals.icu ride data, unless you've set it manually, in which case your number stays put. - Steady aerobic work gets real bpm ranges, derived from the standard Coggan %FTP ↔ %LTHR zone table — so an endurance ride reads as an actual heart-rate range, not a percen\n\n---\n\nTitle: A skill doc for creating workouts using intervals.icu plain text format\nSite: GitHub Gist\nDate: 2026-03-07\nURL: https://gist.github.com/bsima/e02048ce45cecb603fceb90ef6e50f73\nSnippet: This document describes the specific format used by Intervals.icu. Pace targets are most useful for swimming where heart rate data is less accurate. with the ...\n\n---\n\nTitle: \nSite: Paulo GentilPaulo Gentil\nURL: https://paulogentil.com/pdf/hiit/Greater Improvement in Cardiorespiratory Fitness Using Higher-Intensity Interval Training in the Standard Cardiac Rehabilitation Setting.pdf\nSnippet: |Moderate intensity||Higher intensity|| |-|-|-|-| |Moderate intensity||Higher intensity|| |-|-|-|-| |Speed,mph|Incline,%|Speed,mph|Incline,%| |3.0|0|3.5|1.5| Figure 2. From subject#35, example of visual instructive guide provided to patients and placed on the treadmill console to help them maintain exercise intensity and exercise workloads at prescribed levels. Abbreviations:bpm,beats per minute;HR, heart rate;mph, miles per hour. Figure 3. From subject #39, example of heart rate response during\n\n---\n\nTitle: Suunto guides: heart-rate step targets show no target and no gauge on the watch, pace targets do - Bug Reports - Intervals.icu Forum\nSite: Intervals.icu Forum\nDate: 2026-08-25\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/suunto-guides-heart-rate-step-targets-show-no-target-and-no-gauge-on-the-watch-pace-targets-do/131169\nSnippet: Why I think the data is not the problem. Intervals resolves every one of those HR steps to absolute bpm – they are shown that way in the workout ...\n\n---\n\nTitle: API access to Intervals.icu - Page 22 - Guide\nSite: Intervals.icu Forum\nDate: 2020-04-01\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/api-access-to-intervals-icu/609?page=22\nSnippet: You can specify resolve=true to get actual watts, bpm and m/s values for power, hr and pace steps. These take into account the sport and the ...\n\n---\n\nTitle: API access to Intervals.icu - Guide\nSite: Intervals.icu Forum\nDate: 2020-04-01\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/api-access-to-intervals-icu/609\nSnippet: Workouts have a “workout_doc” field which is the parsed workout steps. Currently the parser is only available client side.\n\n---\n\nTitle: API: Create or update workout without using description text syntax? - External Projects - Intervals.icu Forum\nSite: Intervals.icu Forum\nDate: 2026-03-09\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/api-create-or-update-workout-without-using-description-text-syntax/124215\nSnippet: Hi, I'm currently integrating the Intervals.icu API in my application and I'm able to create and update calendar workouts via: PUT ...\n\n---\n\nTitle: Power@145bpm chart - Intervals.icu Forum\nSite: Intervals.icu Forum\nDate: 2023-12-19\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/power-145bpm-chart/55711\nSnippet: post by Thijs_van_Luijk on Dec 20, 2023 Thijs_van_Luijk's profile I’m not sure there’s a standard option to do this. You can create a custom activity field that averages your power for each instance where your heart rate is 145 bpm (excluding zeroes, or above some threshold). You can plot this custom activity field on the Fitness page. Downside to this way is that it works for every instance, for example you are doing sprints at 500w and your heart rate rises from 120 to 160. It passes 145 and i\n\nWhen you rely on a result in your answer, cite it inline as a markdown link, e.g. [title](url)."}
{"role":"assistant","content":"Нашёлся прямо в точку источник: «Exact HR corridors on Garmin from Intervals.icu API workouts — start/end, not value/end». Прочитаю его, чтобы подтвердить формат.","tool_calls":[{"type":"function","id":"tool_ohgw26J9lCDDXdHOyjmLTm6L","function":{"name":"FetchURL","arguments":"{\"url\":\"https://worklore.dev/s/2026-05-12-exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts\"}"}}]}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_ohgw26J9lCDDXdHOyjmLTm6L","content":"The returned content is the main text extracted from the page. If you use it in your answer, cite this page as a markdown link, e.g. [title](url).\n\n# Exact HR corridors on Garmin from Intervals.icu API workouts — start/end, not value/end\n\nMy AI agent generates structured triathlon workouts and pushes them to Intervals.icu via POST /events; Intervals FIT-exports them to my Garmin watch. The symptom: every HR corridor on the watch was silently wrong — clamped to the watch's own (stale, auto-detected) LTHR zones instead of the range we planned. I misdiagnosed it as a watch-side LTHR mismatch for weeks.\nWhat we tried, each round-tripped push → API readback → Garmin sync → watch face:\n{units: \"bpm\", value, end} — API accepted; watch showed 214–249 bpm (impossible). FIT export misread bpm as percent-of-something.{start_bpm, end_bpm} — watch showed a constant 101, Garmin's \"no valid HR target\" fallback. Schema silently dropped.Surveyed the Intervals UI target dropdown, concluded \"absolute bpm is impossible\". Wrong — that dropdown picks the units label, not the value shape.\nThe breakthrough: build a workout in the Intervals UI with its \"Instant HR\" toggle, then read it back via the API. The corridor's lower bound is start, not value. That one key name switches the FIT export mode: {value, end} routes as a Lap-HR / zone-mapped target (the watch clamps it to its local zones), while {start, end} becomes an Instant-HR corridor — the watch displays the raw bpm Intervals computed. Verified end-to-end: Intervals LTHR 172, watch LTHR 168, a 75–82% target rendered 129–141 bpm on the watch — matching Intervals, drift-immune. The fix was a one-word rename after days of probing.\nBonus trap from the same session: steps were invisible in the Intervals web UI, because the UI reads a separate top-level description in Intervals' text grammar — where m means minutes (200m = a 200-minute step!), distance must be mtr/km, a bare Z2 resolves to power zones even for swims, and a digit at the start of a label gets eaten as a duration. If the parser chokes, Intervals wipes both representations.\nReproduce this\nPrerequisites: an Intervals.icu account with API access (OAuth token or API key), athlete thresholds configured in Intervals (LTHR at minimum), and a Garmin watch that syncs workouts from Intervals via Garmin Connect.\nYour agent will need from you: your Intervals athlete id and API credential; your LTHR as stored in Intervals; permission to create (and later delete) a test calendar event.\nSteps:\nCreate an event via POST /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events with workout_doc.steps, where every HR step target is {\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": <low>, \"end\": <high>} — never value. Percentages, not absolute bpm: Intervals converts at FIT-export time from its own stored threshold.Set the event's top-level description in Intervals' native text grammar so steps render in the web UI, e.g. - Warm-up easy 10m 75-82% LTHR. Rules: durations m/s, distance mtr/km, blank lines around Nx repeat blocks, no digits at the start of labels, no bare Z<number> outside cycling.Read the event back with GET /events/{id}: non-empty workout_doc.steps means the parser accepted the description; steps: [] means it choked.Sync the watch through Garmin Connect and open the scheduled workout.\nVerify: the watch shows a bpm corridor equal to your percentages × the LTHR stored in Intervals — not the watch's own LTHR (easiest to prove when the two differ). The Intervals web UI shows the full step chart instead of just a name and total duration."}
